\ ff-test: lines
\ One exception mechanism: every error, THROW, ABORT and QUIT unwinds the
\ same way — through `execute`, deferred words and nested evaluation.
\
\ Errors used to be flags only the outer interpreter looked at: a word
\ that failed inside `execute` or a deferred word let its caller run on,
\ `catch` saw THROW but no engine error, and an uncaught THROW left a flag
\ behind that fooled the next, unrelated `catch`.

: p 32 emit . ;
: bad drop ;
: via-execute ['] bad execute 99 p ;
via-execute
defer hook
' bad is hook
: via-defer hook 99 p ;
via-defer

\ A THROW skips the rest of every word it passes through.
: t1 1 throw ;
: u1 ['] t1 execute 55 p ;
: v1 ['] u1 catch p cr ;
v1

\ Engine errors reach `catch` as their ANS codes, with the stack rolled
\ back to where `catch` was.
' bad catch p cr
: dz 1 0 / ;
' dz catch p cr
: nest3 1 2 3 dz ;
' nest3 catch p depth p cr

\ Uncaught: the host gets FF_ERR_APPLICATION for a code the engine
\ doesn't define, the mapped error for one it does — and nothing stale.
5 throw
: ok 7 ;
' ok catch p p cr
-4 throw

\ ABORT and ABORT" are THROW -1 / -2: catchable, and an uncaught one
\ resets the engine.
: ab abort ;
' ab catch p cr
1 2 abort
depth p cr
: chk abort" "not now" ;
' chk catch p cr
chk

\ QUIT ends the line without an error and passes every catch.
1 2 quit 3 4
depth p cr
: q quit ;
: tq ['] q execute 1 p ;
tq
: cq ['] q catch 1 p ;
cq
clear "5 quit 6" evaluate 7
depth p p cr

\ `evaluate` pushes the THROW code that ended it, as `catch` would, and
\ the error goes no further.
clear "zork" evaluate p cr
"1 0 /" evaluate p cr
"8 throw" evaluate p cr
: ev "1 0 /" evaluate ;
' ev catch p p cr
